# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 2 on bagisto, TWO-STEP PROBE
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the
# order of scenarios/bagisto.yaml (the judge pairs by name). This is a RE-QUESTION: the
# anchor is unchanged and the question has moved, so this rubric is rewritten to score the
# new axis and not the old one.
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THIS RUBRIC CHANGED WITH IT
# Attempt 1 scored the ROUTE by which a site came to be holding the catalogue record. It was
# measured at baseline 0.6471 / sense 0.7647 on `dependents`: the plain arm's accessor sweep
# printed most of the holding sites with their lines already attached, and ten of seventeen
# rows were cited by both arms. This attempt scores something the sweep's output cannot
# state: WHICH kind-specific slice a site owns. Every record here carries a KIND as a plain
# string in one of its own columns; the class that speaks for that kind is assembled at
# runtime out of that string, so no site names what it will actually get. The sites that
# matter are siblings of each other written one per kind, and none of them says which kind it
# is for. A list of every place the record is touched does not distinguish them.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight: coverage and placement are one question. The
# scored set is seventeen sites over eleven areas, and they are siblings without a shared
# abstraction - one owner per kind-specific sub-collection, one per-kind answer to a question
# every kind is asked, one branch per stored-kind value. The failure this criterion exists to
# catch is an answer that lists the record's consumers instead of its kind-slice owners: the
# storefront and checkout read path touches these records constantly and owns nothing, and an
# answer full of it has answered the previous ticket rather than this one.
#
# WHY specificity demands the line AND the routine AND the named slice: a file is not an
# answer. Several scored files hold more than one candidate line, the sibling repositories
# are near-identical to each other and differ only in WHICH collection they own, and a row
# that says "handles product data" has not named a slice. There is a second failure specific
# to this contract: for many rows the kind decision is not taken on the cited line at all -
# it is taken at the runtime lookup on the record, or in a static helper, or by a branch a
# few lines up - so a row that pins the site without locating the decision it rests on has
# given the maintainer half of what the rework needs.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the write path and the per-kind base explicitly: they are the free
# half, both arms reach them in one lookup, they do not score, and pinning them in step 1
# puts the discriminator on the far side of them. What step 1 does NOT ask for is the
# enumeration of the far side: measured on the neighbouring repo in this vertical, asking
# step 1 for the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together, because the plain arm
# wrote its covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2.
#
# WHY uncertainty is worth its weight here: memorization_probe returns ok:false at recall
# 0.417 on this record's own member surface (30 of 72 members named closed-book, samples
# 0.0 / 0.444 / 0.431). Every one of those members sits in the non-scoring contract group, so
# recitation buys nothing on the discriminator - but it makes confident assertion cheap, and
# asserting that a site owns a slice for a kind it does not is worse than an admitted gap.
# The clone is a single squashed commit, so nothing here can be churn-dated; the judge should
# reward an answer that says which lines it actually opened.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent about to rework the catalogue record this store platform hangs off.
  Every one of these records has a KIND, stored as a plain string in one of the record's own
  columns. At runtime that string is turned into the name of a class that speaks for the kind
  - what it costs, whether it can be sold, whether stock applies, which extra collections of
  sub-rows it may carry. After the change, kinds are declared up front, a record can no longer
  produce a behaviour object out of a stored string, and a sub-collection that only some kinds
  carry stops being reachable from the record unless the kind declares it. Score for that
  agent, not a human reader and not a docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what
  the record is today: what it relates outward to, how it answers for a field that was never
  declared on it, and - the centre of the ticket - the exact line where a stored kind becomes
  a class, how that class name is arrived at, where the mapping lives, what happens when
  nothing matches, and how the object gets a handle back on the record; plus the base every
  per-kind class extends and the declared layer whose job is to make and change these records.
  Second, and this is what decides the answer: every place that has taken ownership of a slice
  only some kinds have.
  The distinction the whole ticket turns on is between USING a record and OWNING a slice of
  one. Reading a plain field off a record and passing it on is use: it survives the change
  untouched and it is not an answer. Ownership is a line somewhere else entirely - in the
  middle of a routine about saving an option payload, indexing a price, validating a shipment,
  rendering an admin grid, running an import - where the code either writes and prunes a
  collection that only certain kinds carry, or takes a branch decided by the stored kind
  value, or delegates its work to the object that speaks for the kind and would be left
  holding nothing once that object stops being reachable.
  These owners are siblings and none of them knows it. Each was written for a single kind,
  none of them says which, and there is no shared abstraction listing them - so the set can
  only be assembled by working out, per site, which slice it is the owner of. The class a site
  will actually get is never named at the site: it is assembled at runtime from the stored
  value, so no line of source connects the two.
  Most of this layer sits in parts of the codebase whose own subject has nothing to do with
  the catalogue, and the parts that do sit near the record are its peripheral write and index
  helpers rather than the record's own file. An answer dense in the record's own file and its
  obvious consumers, and thin everywhere else, has enumerated inside one search scope and
  mistaken it for the codebase.
  Every site must be pinned to the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, with the
  kind-specific slice it owns named concretely, with where the kind decision it rests on is
  taken, and with one phrase on what breaks if nothing is done.

steps:
- name: Map the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the record presented as one connected picture - what it relates outward to, how it
        answers for a field that was never declared on it, and how it turns its stored kind
        into the object that speaks for that kind - rather than as a list of routines or a
        roll of places the name appears? Two of those matter most and they are the two the
        ticket turns on: that asking the record for an undeclared field SUCCEEDS, because a
        lookup is done behind the scenes rather than a property being read; and that the class
        which speaks for a kind is arrived at by building a configuration key out of a value
        stored on the record, so nothing in source names which class comes back. An answer
        that locates the lookup line, says how the class name is derived from the stored
        value, says where the mapping from value to class is written down, says what happens
        when nothing matches, and says how the object gets a handle back on the record, has
        understood the ticket.
        The step then asks for the base that every per-kind class extends and for the declared
        write path, at file:line, with a phrase on what each is responsible for and how the
        write path itself goes through the per-kind object. This part is deliberately the easy
        part and it is asked for on purpose - it is the half whose relationship to the record
        is written down where anyone can see it, and the next step is about the other kind -
        so score coverage and location here, not difficulty.
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: what it looks like when
        some unrelated piece of code has taken ownership of a slice only some kinds have, and
        why nothing in that file's shape gives it away. Score whether that is characterised
        correctly, with one located example. Do NOT reward an exhaustive enumeration of those
        places: it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a better answer here than
        one accurate observation. Could the next step act from this orientation with targeted
        lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine
        or class it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the
        character offset falls. Where the answer describes the stored kind becoming a class,
        is the line that actually performs the lookup located, and is the file holding the
        mapping from stored value to class located too - or is the mechanism asserted from a
        method name and a familiar framework convention? Same test for the override that
        answers for an undeclared field, for the base of the per-kind family, and for the
        layer that creates and updates records.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part carry one phrase on what it contributes - what asking for an undeclared
        field actually does, what decides which per-kind object comes back and what happens
        when nothing matches, what the write path is responsible for that the record is not -
        and does the answer connect those facts to why merely using a record and owning a
        kind-specific slice of one have completely different fates after the change, rather
        than restating the list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name
        or a familiar framework convention? This matters more than usual here: closed-book
        this model can already name a third of this record's member surface, so it invites
        confident recitation, and saying which parts were actually opened is worth more here
        than elsewhere. This platform also gives the record's short name to an empty marker
        interface, to a swap-in proxy class and to several unrelated classes in other
        packages - does the answer show it settled which one it was looking at where that
        mattered? Is anything the session could not resolve named as such rather than smoothed
        over, and is the observation about code owning a kind-specific slice offered as
        something it established rather than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND PLACEMENT, and the two are one question here. Is every site listed
        separately, each pinned to its own routine, with the kind-specific slice it owns named
        concretely? A place named with no slice is half a row: the remedy depends entirely on
        WHAT that site owns and whether the kind can declare it, and that is the only decision
        this ticket makes.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on: using a record versus owning a slice of
        one. The record itself, the layer whose job is to create and update these records, and
        the base of the per-kind family are NOT answers here - they were pinned in the
        previous step, and an answer built out of them has answered the easy half. Nor is a
        place that reads a plain field off a record and passes it on: the storefront and
        checkout read path does that constantly and owns nothing, and an answer made of it has
        listed consumers rather than owners.
        Score whether the answer found ownership of more than one shape - a routine that
        writes and prunes a sub-collection only certain kinds carry, a per-kind class giving
        its own answer to a question every kind is asked, a branch taken directly on the
        stored kind value, a site that delegates its whole result to the object that speaks
        for the kind, a helper that hard-codes which collections a kind may have. An answer in
        which every row looks the same has either found only one band or has stopped reading
        at the first hit. Does it notice, at least once, that several of these sites are
        SIBLINGS of each other - the same job written once per kind with nothing linking them?
        Score WHERE the answer looked, and also HOW FINELY. These sites sit in parts of the
        codebase whose own subject has nothing to do with the record's own file - the
        peripheral repositories that own one sub-collection each, the price and flat index
        builders, the admin edit endpoints and grid payloads, form validation, a view-render
        listener in an add-on package, a data importer - and a single file can hold more than
        one. An answer dense in the record's own file and its obvious consumers, or one that
        names files rather than routines, has stopped at the shallow edge; that is the exact
        failure this criterion names.
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's sites listed individually, and is
        what was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every site carry an exact file:line, AND the name of the routine it sits in, AND
        the kind-specific slice it owns named concretely, AND where the kind decision it rests
        on is taken? All four, per row. A bare filename is not specific, and on this contract
        it is actively wrong: one file can hold several sites and only some are in play. "It
        handles product data" is not a named slice - which collection, or which kind's branch,
        or which delegated behaviour. A run of places named together under one shared reason
        with no location on any of them is the failure this criterion exists to catch, and it
        does not become specific because the reason is correct.
        Is the located line the OWNERSHIP rather than the declaration? A relation method on
        the record, a repository's model() declaration, a docblock type or a constructor
        parameter is where a relationship was declared; the site is the line where code whose
        job is something else writes, branches on, or delegates a kind-specific slice. Where a
        file contains both, or contains several sites in different routines, citing the wrong
        one is citing the wrong line, not a rounding error.
        Where the kind decision is NOT taken on the cited line - because it happens at the
        runtime lookup on the record, in a static helper, or in a branch elsewhere - does the
        answer locate it, or does it leave the row pointing at a consequence with no cause?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Is the phrase per row a consequence of THAT site, rather than a restatement that the
        place uses the record? "Breaks if the record changes" is not a justification. "It owns
        the whole lifecycle of a collection only one kind carries, so after the change that
        collection has to be declared by the kind or this routine has nothing to prune", "it
        branches on the stored value directly rather than through the object, so it keeps
        working and silently disagrees with the kind", "it delegates its entire result to the
        object that speaks for the kind, so it is left holding nothing once that object stops
        being reachable", "it hard-codes which collections a kind may have inside a private
        resolver, so the allowed set is stated in two places that can drift" are. Does the
        answer say, at least in aggregate, which sites can be re-expressed as a declared kind
        capability and which cannot?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer distinguish sites it traced by reading from ones it inferred from a
        name, a familiar convention, or a memory of this platform? That distinction matters
        more than usual here: a routine named after a kind is not evidence that it owns that
        kind's slice, a local variable named after the record is not evidence the record is
        what is in it, and several unrelated classes in this codebase carry the same short
        name as the record, as do an empty marker interface and a swap-in proxy. Attributing a
        slice to the wrong kind, or to the wrong one of those classes, is worse than an
        admitted gap - does the answer show it settled that where it mattered? Is coverage
        claimed honestly - which areas it swept and which it did not reach - and where it
        could not tell which slice a routine owns, does it say so instead of picking one
        silently?
